<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sarsa Dengel</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1550–<a href="/years/1597">1597</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solomonic-dynasty">Solomonic dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sarsa Dengel was Emperor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a> in the second half of the sixteenth century. According to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adal-sultanate">Adal Sultanate</a> profile, he defeated and executed the Adal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-v-of-adal">Sultan Muhammad V</a> in 1577. This defeat, together with others, permanently ended the Adal Sultanate as a military power.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sarsa-dengel.jpg" alt="Sarsa Dengel" width="302" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: ZemenfesKidus · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Western_kings_on_Horseback_(detail).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Sarsa Dengel, a member of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solomonic-dynasty">Solomonic dynasty</a>, was the son of Emperor Menas and Empress Admas Mogasa. He was barely fourteen when the Shewan commanders of the army and the Dowager Empress elected him king, and he took the throne name Malak Sagad I. On coming of age he had to put down revolts by his cousins, first Hamalmal, defeated at the Battle of Endagabatan, and then Fasil. During his rule he moved the centre of the empire from Shewa to Begemder.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>In the 1570s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oromo">Oromo</a> groups were moving north. In 1572 he fought off a Borana raid near Lake Zway, and in 1574 he gathered an army at Gind Beret and drove the Oromo out of Wej and Maya. When the Adal Sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-ibn-nasir">Muhammad ibn Nasir</a> declared a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jihad">jihad</a> and campaigned in Hadiya, Sarsa Dengel defeated him at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-webi-river">Battle of Webi River</a>. When the local ruler, Bahr Negus Yeshaq, allied with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/debarwa">Debarwa</a>, the emperor defeated them at the Battle of Addi Qarro in Tigray, where Yeshaq and the Ottoman commander Ahmad Pasha were killed. The garrison of Debarwa surrendered, and he had its mosque and fort destroyed.</p><p>After a coronation at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aksumite-empire">Aksum</a> he campaigned from 1580 against the Beta Israel in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/semien">Semien</a>, declining to turn back when the Borana raided Shewa, Waj and Damot, to the frustration of his officials. In 1586 the Borana invaded Gojjam, and he then forced the Dawé Oromo in Wej to flee south. In 1587 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> advanced from Hirgigo and retook <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/debarwa">Debarwa</a>; he drove them back, raided the fort at Hirgigo and killed its commander, Kadawert Pasha, after which they withdrew and handed the port to a local Balaw chief.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>According to his chronicle, monks warned him on his last campaign, against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oromo">Oromo</a> in Damot, not to eat fish from a certain river; he ate it, fell sick and died. He was buried in the Medhane Alem church on Rema Island. Historians differ over his reign. J. Spencer Trimingham hails him as one of the great warrior-kings of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopian-empire">Ethiopian Empire</a> and Franz Dombrowski credits him with ending the Ottoman threat, while Mordechai Abir and Richard Pankhurst argue that his northern campaigns left the south open to the Oromo migration, which Abir calls a turning point towards the empire's decline.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sarsa Dengel” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Emperor <b>Sarsa Dengel</b>'s defeat of Sultan Muhammad ibn Nasir at the Battle of Webi River in 1576, followed by the sultan's capture and execution, ended Adal as a military power, and in 1577 the dynasty withdrew to the oasis of Aussa.</blockquote><a href="/empire/adal-sultanate">Adal Sultanate · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Inland campaigns failed after <b>Sarsa Dengel's</b> victories in the 1570s and 1580s.</blockquote><a href="/state/habesh-eyalet">Habesh Eyalet</a></li><li><blockquote>Its rulers paid tribute in gold to the Ethiopian Empire, and in the late 16th century, under Emperor <b>Sarsa Dengel</b>, its king and court were baptised.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-ennarea">Kingdom of Ennarea</a></li><li><blockquote>In the 1560s and 1570s Bahr Negash Yeshaq allied with the Ottoman Habesh Eyalet against the emperor <b>Sarsa Dengel</b>, showing the province's frequent independence.</blockquote><a href="/state/medri-bahri">Medri Bahri</a></li></ul></section>
